    {
     "author": {
      "accountId": "6089a55c52a3d400690692f7",
      "accountType": "atlassian",
      "active": true,
      "avatarUrls": {
       "16x16": "https://avatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net/6089a55c52a3d400690692f7/7a62c587-b0f1-4cd4-982c-21b3837dc64a/16",
       "24x24": "https://avatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net/6089a55c52a3d400690692f7/7a62c587-b0f1-4cd4-982c-21b3837dc64a/24",
       "32x32": "https://avatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net/6089a55c52a3d400690692f7/7a62c587-b0f1-4cd4-982c-21b3837dc64a/32",
       "48x48": "https://avatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net/6089a55c52a3d400690692f7/7a62c587-b0f1-4cd4-982c-21b3837dc64a/48"
      },
      "displayName": "Ralf Claussnitzer",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=6089a55c52a3d400690692f7",
      "timeZone": "America/Halifax"
     },
     "body": "<p>From looking at how the Driver application is configured I assume that all extensions simply had to be JARs on the classpath. These would provide the annotated classes (<tt>@Configuration</tt>) and a RouteBuilder for being picked up by the Spring classpath scanner. All that's needed for this to work is changing the Java command in <tt>entrypoint.sh</tt> to pick-up additional JARs. They could for example reside in <tt>camel-toolbox-config/</tt>.</p>\n\n<p>It’s still unclear to me what dependencies would be required for developing and testing extensions outside of the <tt>fcrepo-camel-toolbox</tt> project.</p>",
     "created": "16/Feb/22 10:14 AM",
     "id": "22599",
     "jsdPublic": true,
     "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/issue/13684/comment/22599",
